Date: Thu, 1 Jul 93 9:15:52 PDT
From: Eli Brandt <ebrandt@jarthur.Claremont.EDU>
Subject: Flying snakes

Eric Marsh said:
> If a species of squirrel or lizard finds that jumping increases its
> chances of survival, then adaptations to allow it to jump further such
> as the ability to glide are logical. However I am unaware of any tree
> dwelling species of snake that jumps in any way. Thus, it seems to
> me that the evolution of a flying snake is quite a "leap."

There is a presently-hip way of describing evolutionary search that
may make this look less peculiar.  Think of a search within a search:
the top-level search is in genome space, but it has an inner loop
searching in behavior space.  The genotype constrains the range of
behavior which is available, and alters the payoffs.  <Two of the
three people I've seen describing this did a funky thing with their
arm and wrist at this point, to demonstrate the degrees of freedom.
I'll refrain.>

So what?  Well, this suggests that you don't need to start with
jumping snakes to evolve gliding snakes.  Slim snakes don't jump out
of trees, because this behavior in their case results in high-
velocity impact.  But if some mutant snake happens to end up with
an air-catching belly, it will discover that jumping is now a useful
behavior.

This description of evolution is relevant to the present discussion
of GA's as well.  This sort of interpretation, by behavior-space
search, appears to be more robust than the direct approach on
certain toy problems.

   Eli   ebrandt@jarthur.claremont.edu

