In <9306301656.AA21486@wookumz.gnu.ai.mit.edu>, pmetzger@lehman.com (Perry E. Metzger)  wrote:
> 
> 
> "James A. Donald" says:
> > There is an obvious and basic error in Drexler's work.  
> > 
> > Drexler proposes to make nanomachines of large rigid
> > molecules, but for large rigid molecules the glass point is
> > well above the decomposition point, meaning static
> > friction is so high that they break before they shift.
> 
> Okay, I'll bite. Why don't the molecular systems in the human body,
> typically made of large rigid molecules, fail for this reason?

Your facts are even worse than Drexler's.  As Drexler says,
(read him) biological molecules are flexible.  The most
rigid molecule normally used in the body is DNA, and in its
active state it continually puffs, (locally separates in
small areas) due to brownian motion.  In its inactive
state, packed for storage, it is truly rigid, but that is
irrelevant.

> > People have made Drexler style micro machines etched out
> > of silicon, using line widths of several micrometers. 
> > When enough force is applied to make them move, they
> > shatter, because static friction overwhelms mechanical
> > strength.
> 
> Now, this is bullshit for two reasons. The first is that silicon
> micro machines are NOT nanotechnology, and the second is that I've met
> several people involved in micro machines that the things do work, and
> quite well. In other words, your whole paragraph is just plain wrong.

Micro machines work when they are large enough, or when
they have NO SLIDING SURFACES hence no static friction.
All commercially useful micro machines that I am aware of
have NO SLIDING SURFACES.  Micro engines that rotate,
hence require sliding surfaces, are not very micro.

> > The Drexler approach has been tried and, as predicted, has
> > failed.  The smaller you get, the larger static friction
> > becomes relative to mechanical strength
> 
> Huh?

Basic physics.

Van der Waals forces become stronger and stronger at small
separations. The increase is very rapid.  A crude rule of
thumb is r^(-6)

Take a length of fishing line:  Imagine a cut lengthwise
through it.  This arbitrary dividing surface has the same
gap as the gap between a Drexler axle and a Drexler axle
housing.  The two halves are held together by the same
forces as make a Drexler axle stick, van der Waals forces.  
(Fishing line is covalently bonded along its length, van der 
Waals bonded perpendicular to its length.) Now slide the 
two halves apart.

Small problem with static friction between the two halves?

