Delete this if you believe in not reading posts from people who haven't done
the reading:  _Mea_maxima_culpa_!
 
I see that AIT has to do with characterizing the complexity of a string in
terms of the length of a program required to create it.  I see also some
issues having to do with keeping the discussion of complexity as environment-
free as possible.  I haven't seen any discussion of the nature of the
"string-describing machine" on which these programs are imagined to run,
or of the limits and nature of the programming constructs which are allowed
to be used to write those programs.  It seems to me that once you admit
program length as a figure of merit, it no longer suffices to talk about
programs running on Turing machines and exploit the reducibility of fancier
hardware thereto, because programs for Turing machines sometimes tend to
be looong... .  So I suspect that the level of complexity of a string
depends on the hardware and software available for the program that describes
it, and if I am right, I was wondering whether AIT had found any particularly
neat ramifications of this idea.
 
               -- Jay Freeman, who really would like to do the reading ...
 
