Date: Thu, 1 Jul 93 10:49:54 PDT
From: Eli Brandt <ebrandt@jarthur.Claremont.EDU>
Subject: AIT VirtSem: Critical Bits, Structure, and Information

> From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
> - a poker hand is often used to illustrate outcomes, "important" outcomes
> (like a royal flush), and the basics of probability. It is true that any
> specific hand is as likely as one of the royal flushes (10-J-Q-K-A in one
> suit), but "nearly all" poker hands are "less interesting" and are not much
> changed by twiddling one of the cards.

Let me take a simpler system: a string of 1000 Bernoulli
(random-bit) variables.  Consider three sorts of strings:
1) an uncorrelated string.  Random crud, the analog of your "gas" example.
2) all-ones.  A "crystal".
3) a string encoding a strategy for a simple game.

Algorithmic complexity pings on (1) and (3), but not (2).
A "structure" or "bit importance" measure pings on (2) and (3), not (1).
So we can describe "genuine complexity" as, say, the product of
the above two measures.  Is this estimator on the right track?

Incidentally, we need a way to measure "structure".  Looking at the
above three examples, let's consider the size of the set of
"functionally-similar" strings.  For (1), it's approximately 2^1000.
For (2) -- well, there's exactly one all-one string, but there
might be, say, 2^20 strings that we want to call "crystal analogues".
For (3), there might be 2^5 strings encoding that particular
strategy, or more if we allow similar strategies, or...
So now let's define "structural entropy" as
	lg |set of similar strings|/|set of all strings|
This does approximately what we want, but is a little simplistic.
It requires us to define a universal set and speak in terms of
"functional string classes", which is a little tacky.  If in "bit
criticality" we want to capture the sensitivity to a particular
type of mutation (point, frame, whatever), this definition falls
flat.

   Eli   ebrandt@jarthur.claremont.edu

