Subject: AIT VirtSem: Critical Bits, Structure, and Information

I commend Hal for his clearly stated objection to including
ideas of function in an expansion of AIT toward studies of
real complex systems.  I am not willing to give up the
necessity yet, though, and have a couple of comments on his
note.

First, as Tim has pointed out, we always take some "environmental"
concerns into account in the nature of the UTM we choose to use
to do the programming.  This pales to insignificance on infinite
strings, but is of some real importance for finite strings.

> modern view towards something more resembling traditional information theory.
> The introduction of fitness landscapes, and stipulations that the objects
> under study (organisms, or Go programs) have to have a certain amount of 
> fitness in competition with others, add a great deal of intellectual baggage
> to an otherwise clean theory.

Yes, I think this is true.  If that baggage can't be packed efficiently,
the effort isn't likely to get very far.

> We may agree that in "pure" AIT a blended frog (one whose molecules have been
> given random positions) has greater information content (at the molecular
> level) than a living frog.  Derek proposes to "fix" this by requiring that
> the frog under discussion succeeds in a competitive environment.

well... how about "external evaluation mechanism"?  Competition, per
se, isn't necessary -- though evolution uses it.
 
> Similarly, the suggestion that the complexity of an entity depends on its
> environment is out of keeping with the spirit of AIT, in my view.  A frog
> would be just as complex in a glass jar on the moon as it would in a pond
> on earth.

That's certainly true -- but the problem is still that the current
version of AIT gives the "wrong" answer too often.  Note that the
frog on the moon probably has higher logical depth than the one in
the pond!

> Taking the poker example, AIT will already tell us that four aces has less
> randomness than a typical hand.  To try to go beyond this and find a measure
> which gave higher complexity to four aces plus a king than to four aces
> plus a queen (on the theory that poker hands of higher value should have
> greater complexity), based on the fitness of these hands in the poker
> environment, is not a useful move, IMO.

This I don't understand.  You like that a four-ace hand has distinction
in AIT.  Why?  The point of mentioning a four-ace hand is that it's
a really good poker hand, right?  If that's not the motivation, then
why mention poker at all?

> In general, I feel that we should avoid as much as possible bringing in
> arbitrary, human-recognized measures in judging complexity and randomness.
> We should stick to measures which can be formalized, which are objective,
> and which do not require an a priori recognition of purpose and function.

This I agree with heartily.  But I have a question for you, Hal:  why
would you like AIT extended (if you would at all)?  That is, what
question would you like to answer with an extended AIT?  Personally,
my largest interest is rather practical: I'd like to do some sort of
calculation of the difficulty of producing objects that have certain
functional properties (doing classification tasks, thinking, self-
reproducing, making some kind of widget, etc) -- by purposive or
evolutionary means.  That's why this connection interests me.

Suppose we do take "life" as some sort of thing that an "objective"
AIT should be maximum for.  Well, I very much doubt that we'd even
agree on what life is without bringing in functional concepts, so
how are we to decide how well an AIT measure performs?

derek

