Date: Thu, 1 Jul 93 11:40:32 -0700
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: AIT VirtSem: The Game of "Go" as an Example

>Isn't go a terminating game?  If so, there are a finite number of
>games, as the player has a finite set of options at each move.
>The best strategy is then to search the game tree.
>(Sorry.  I have to stop talking to these pure math types.)

>   Eli   ebrandt@jarthur.claremont.edu


But the "search tree" is upwards of 10^700 games! Anyone who proposed
exhaustive search of the Go game tree as a realistic approach would be
dismissed out of hand (I'm not trying to insult Eli, just note that
exhaustive search is not an option in the real world. Evolution "knows"
this, and we are learning it as well.)

Even if one wants to only look ahead, say, 10 moves (which is demonstrably
not enough when facing expert players), there are something like 10^20 or
more branches to consider (depends on where one is in the game, of course).
Analyzing one branch every microsecond (vastly shorter than it would
actually take with existing computers), the 10^20 branches could then be
analyzed in several million years!

As Nick Szabo noted, Go is a game of "satisficing," not optimization.Time
is of the essence. (The rabbit being chased by a fox dashes toward his
hiding hole, computing a "good enough" path, not an optimal path. Sometimes
he wins, sometimes he loses. But both the chaser and chasee have
constraints (speed, energy, endurance, etc.) that make it a complicated
game, for which "optimal solutions" are intractable to compute---and Nature
hasn't even tried.)

What do large numbers like 10^700 mean? (And it is completely trivial to
find situations in which the relevant numbers are _much_ larger. Go played
on a slightly larger board, for example.)

This becomes a semi-religious point, in a sense, in that I believe
computations involving exact calculation of 10^200 things (let alone 10^700
things!) will never, ever, be done in our Universe. I know Mike Price has
challenged me to prove this, pointing out that I don't know--that none of
us knows--that such computations will never be done.

True enough, in some sense, which is why it boils down to an eschatological
(not be be confused with scatological, though there are similarities) view
about the ultimate nature of computation.

>From what we know, there are about 10^70 elementary particles in the
Universe...all the galaxies, all the stars, the quasars, the intergalactic
dust, even the "dark matter." If each of these elementary particles
(somehow) contained a computer which computed....well, do the calculations
yourself, with your favorite assumptions about cycle time, etc.

(Even "pocket universes" don't buy us much, when talking about large numbers.)

The essence of AI, or one of the essences, is knowing what to consider,
what to ignore, and how to make "good enough" calculations. The
"computational frame problem," as it were.

-Tim May
