Subject: AIT: Logical Depth vs String Complexity

  I've only been following this discussion ocassionally because all my
energy is going into the list software at the moment, but I have a question.

What would you consider the more important information measure for life,
logical depth or string complexity(representational)?

  I think logical depth is a better measure and here's why. What if we
were to take an E. Coli's gene code, run gzip on it, and compress it
50%. Well, we just found a 50% shorter representation of the E. Coli's
gene code, does that make it any more or less complex? I think not.
[note: when I use the word complex, I am using the everyday definition]
The Coli could still perform a marvelously wide range of functions 
(very complex ones) even if its program representation was redundant.

  IMHO, it is far more interesting to study how much computation
is needed to represent life and not how much storage is required.


