Date: Thu, 1 Jul 93 17:06:05 EDT
From: fnerd@smds.com (FutureNerd Steve Witham)
Subject: NANO: Drexler style nanotech #005

James A. Donald <jamesdon@infoserv.com> writes more:
> 
> Hard deterministic machines in a cold vacuum
> cannot work for components smaller than a few micrometers, at least
> they cannot work with components that anyone has designed so far.

I don't know whether this means Drexler's designs can't work for
theoretical reasons, or no working nanomachines have been
*demonstrated* so far...I guess the former.
 
> Drexler proposes to make nanomachines of large rigid
> molecules, but for large rigid molecules the glass point is
> well above the decomposition point, meaning static
> friction is so high that they break before they shift.

I think a couple of us would appreciate a short tutorial on the
concepts of glass point and decomposition point.  But I'm not
sure they'll turn out to be relevant to nanotech, since you
seem to be discussing them in an argument about friction.
On that issue, I must not have spoken clearly enough last time.

The parts of nanomachines have clearances around them.

They never slide against each other.  They pivot and
roll, or they glide through holes with clearance.

By "clearance," I mean an area of vacuum.

There is no friction when nothing rubs against anything.

> Normal macroscopic designs become non functional due to
> static friction at scales of a few micrometers.  People
> have tried them and, as predicted from scaling laws, they
> fail.

All of us agree that normal macroscopic sliding-part designs 
have nothing to do with it.  Exactly placed atoms in careful
choreography are different from roughly sculpted heaps grinding
against each other.  
 
> People have made Drexler style micro machines etched out
> of silicon, using line widths of several micrometers. 

Here maybe the source of our misunderstanding.  Etched
machines are of the rough-sculpted-grinding-heaps school,
and have nothing to do with Drexler's style.

> Designs that will work on a scale a thousand times smaller
> will need to be fundamentally different.  They will look
> nothing like macro machines.

Drexler machines look nothing like micro machines, but in a 
strange way they look like macro machines again, since once
again you know the exact shapes and behaviors of the parts
(atoms), which are once again smooth.

> They will consist of highly flexible molecules floating in
> a very large amount of lubricant, with vastly more
> lubricant than machinery.  The molecules of the lubricant
> need to be as small as possible and to have high solvation
> capability in order to separate the nanostructures. Water
> is probably the best such lubricant.  In other words
> nanomachinery must very strongly resemble living
> protoplasm, and will not resemble rigid clockwork at all.

This is an interesting alternative or roundabout intermediary
to Drexler-style nanotech.  It sounds like you would have to 
waste a lot of mass and volume and...

> movement rates that are small compared to the
> brownian motion.

...time, but when you've got orders of magnitude to spare, 
why not?  But ultimately I think vacuum and lack of contact 
are much more compact, lightweight and well-behaved lubricants 
that support faster motion.

-fnerd

